From 735864f65b86ffa43d1d70efa4cee863123df6f3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 00:28:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] reaper_fw: Service Intercept - send a service to a server of
 your choosing

Owner's case: every LAN device must take NTP from an internal time server, and
only the router and that server may reach external time as a fallback. The
egress half was already expressible (Rules tab, dir=fwd), but the interception
half was not: the existing Forwards feature hardcodes -i "$WANIF", so it can
only translate WAN-INBOUND traffic. This adds the LAN-side direction.

Same record type, not a new one. reaper_fw_nat gains four APPEND-ONLY optional
fields - mode>szone>exempt>snat - so every existing forward keeps working
untouched: a pre-existing record has no field 8, rfw_field() returns false, and
it defaults to mode=forward, which is exactly its old meaning. Verified by
emitting a legacy 8-field record and getting byte-identical output.

  mode=forward    unchanged, WAN-inbound DNAT
  mode=intercept  traffic from a source zone to a port is DNAT'd to a server
  mode=redirect   the same, but to the router itself (-j REDIRECT --to-ports)

Two things a plain DNAT gets wrong, both emitted automatically because an
operator should not have to know them:

  - THE TARGET IS ALWAYS EXEMPT AS A SOURCE (! -s target, plus ! -d target so
    traffic already addressed to it is not re-translated). Without this the
    server's own upstream query is intercepted straight back to itself, so it can
    never sync and the fallback the whole arrangement exists for is dead.
  - THE RETURN PATH. If the server shares a segment with the clients it answers
    them DIRECTLY from its own address and the client discards the reply - it
    expected the address it originally addressed. That needs a MASQUERADE, and it
    must NOT be emitted when the server is on another VLAN (the reply already
    routes back through us, and masquerading would cost the server the client's
    identity for nothing). snat=auto asks the LIVE routing table at apply time
    (ip route get <target>) whether the translated packet would leave by the
    interface it arrived on, and only then emits it. on/off override.

The router needs no exemption at all: PREROUTING never sees locally generated
traffic, which is also why its own fallback keeps working - the same structural
reason a dir=fwd rule cannot lock the router out.

New chain REAPER_FWM in nat POSTROUTING for the return path, with its own hook
and its own teardown. The teardown runs on the rollback path too: a MASQUERADE
left behind after an auto-revert would keep rewriting traffic to a server whose
intercept no longer exists. Appended (not inserted) to POSTROUTING so it runs
after the stock WAN masquerade; it only matches traffic already aimed at an
intercept target, so it cannot shadow anything.

Validation fails CLOSED, and the UI mirrors each refusal so a bad record is
rejected at save rather than silently dropped into syslog: unknown mode (a
record from a newer build must not degrade into an arbitrary forward), a
redirect carrying a target IP, a source zone that resolves to no interface (an
unbound rule would match every interface including the WAN), and an exemption
that does not resolve (applying an intercept without the exemption it was
designed around is worse than not applying it).

UI: a Service Intercept card on the NAT tab, sharing RFW.nat with Forwards -
each card renders only its own rows and skips (rather than filters) the others,
so the array index stays true for delete and edit. The server field and the
return-path selector hide for a redirect instead of reading as ignored settings.
15 tokens RFW_258-272 appended to all 25 dicts in lockstep (6750 -> 6765).

Scope, stated on purpose: IPv4 only, matching the existing if (!v6) gate - on a
dual-stack LAN an intercept is bypassable over IPv6, and the mitigation is a v6
drop rule the Rules tab already does. Port-based, so it cannot catch a device
that fetches time over HTTPS or NTS. DNS is deliberately NOT in scope: DNS
Director already ships, does per-client policy and blocks DoT on 853, which this
would not.

Verified without a build: extracted the real rfw_emit_nat() and compiled it with
stubs over nine records - legacy forward byte-identical; intercept emits both
exclusions, the DNAT, the companion FORWARD accept and the guarded MASQUERADE;
multi-interface zone emits per interface; proto=both expands to tcp+udp with a
distinct REAPER-ICPT log prefix; all four rejections fire with their log lines.
The emitted script passes sh -n. Page: every new element id resolves, every new
token resolves in EN.dict, the inline block parses under node --check, div tags
balance in the tab and the document, ASCII-clean, reaper_langcheck 0 breakers.

Specced in ASUS/SPEC-service-intercept.md. Two deviations from that spec, both
deliberate: an unknown mode is DROPPED rather than treated as a forward (fail
closed - the spec's fallback would emit a DNAT nobody asked for), and the
"refuse an intercept on the WAN zone" check is left out because it needs the
apply-time $WANIF and its outcome is equivalent to a forward, not a hole.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
